<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-12-moving-on" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Moving On!</title>

  <p>
    Congratulations! In this chapter, you crossed the defining threshold of modern software
    engineering, transitioning from procedural decomposition to designing robust, extensible
    systems using object-oriented programming:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Shifting your design mindset:</strong> Moving beyond standalone functions to model
        software as communities of interacting objects that bundle data (state) together with
        operations (behavior).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Establishing rigorous encapsulation and information hiding:</strong> Declaring
        private instance variables to protect internal data and exposing public constructors,
        accessors, and mutators to govern access.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Organizing multi-class solutions:</strong> Structuring classes across separate
        source files in an IDE and visualizing blueprints and instances with UML class diagrams.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Distinguishing static versus instance scope:</strong> Distinguishing between state
        belonging to individual heap objects and shared class-level constants and counters.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Crafting flexible initialization contracts:</strong> Overloading constructors to
        provide multiple ways to instantiate objects, writing copy constructors, and using the
        <c>this</c> reference to resolve variable shadowing and chain constructors.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Mastering the Java memory model:</strong> Visualizing how reference variables on
        the call stack point to dynamic objects in heap memory, untangling reference aliasing,
        contrasting shallow versus deep copies, defending against <c>null</c>, and seeing how
        Java's garbage collector automatically reclaims unreachable memory.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Integrating with the Java Object hierarchy:</strong> Overriding <c>toString()</c>
        to produce informative, human-readable representations and implementing value-based
        <c>equals()</c> methods to compare object contents rather than memory addresses.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Authoring contract-driven Javadoc specifications:</strong> Formally defining class
        invariants in header comments and documenting method contracts without leaking private
        implementation details.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Verifying stateful behavior with Arrange–Act–Assert:</strong> Validating object
        state transitions across mutations, enforcing preconditions with defensive assertions,
        and modeling operational lifecycles using UML state-transition diagrams.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Composing collaborating systems:</strong> Using Class–Responsibility–Collaborator
        (CRC) cards to decompose problem domains, establishing unidirectional <q>has-a</q>
        relationships, and coordinating multiple objects in a complete capstone case study.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    You are now fully equipped to tackle the programming exercises at the end of this chapter.
    Work through the steps: model your problem domain on paper with CRC cards or class diagrams,
    define your class invariants upfront, scaffold your classes with compiling method stubs,
    enforce your preconditions with assertions, build Arrange–Act–Assert unit tests that verify
    both valid transitions and error boundaries, and use your IDE's debugger and memory diagrams
    to trace reference variables whenever you need to inspect object state on the heap!
  </p>
</section>